WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Question 0001: a rising edge detector,
a student-level question.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:10.100
One part: draw the circuit, write it
in SystemVerilog, and draw a timing diagram.

3
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:15.300
A signal sig is synchronous to a clock clk.
What goes inside the box?

4
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:21.067
The goal: a pulse one clock cycle wide
each time sig rises from 0 to 1.

5
00:00:21.767 --> 00:00:25.967
Here is sig from the page's diagram,
ten cycles of the clock.

6
00:00:25.967 --> 00:00:31.567
sig rises twice, so we want two pulses,
each exactly one cycle wide.

7
00:00:31.567 --> 00:00:35.733
Even when sig stays high for four cycles,
the pulse does not stretch.

8
00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:41.533
A combinational circuit sees only sig of now.
In cycle 2 and in cycle 4 it sees 1.

9
00:00:41.533 --> 00:00:47.033
The difference is the cycle before: before cycle 2
sig was 0, and before cycle 4 it was 1.

10
00:00:47.033 --> 00:00:51.033
A change is a comparison of two cycles,
so the circuit must remember the last sig.

11
00:00:51.833 --> 00:00:57.367
A D flip-flop remembers the last value:
at every rising clock edge it samples sig.

12
00:00:57.367 --> 00:01:01.333
Its output, sig_q, is the value of sig
in the previous cycle.

13
00:01:01.333 --> 00:01:06.300
A rising edge: 1 now, and 0 before.
That is sig, and the inverse of sig_q.

14
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:11.600
The output: pulse = sig & ~sig_q.
That is the whole circuit: one memory, two gates.

15
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:17.500
Now let's build the timing diagram,
cycle by cycle, with the same sig.

16
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:22.967
In cycles 0 and 1 sig is low. The reset
starts sig_q at 0, and pulse is 0 too.

17
00:01:22.967 --> 00:01:28.267
In cycle 2 sig rises. The flip-flop still
remembers 0, so pulse = 1 & ~0 = 1.

18
00:01:28.267 --> 00:01:33.433
At the next clock edge sig_q catches the 1,
and ~sig_q = 0 clears pulse.

19
00:01:33.433 --> 00:01:38.400
In cycles 4 and 5 sig is still high,
but so is sig_q: the pulse does not stretch.

20
00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:43.200
In cycle 6 sig falls, and the AND clears at once:
a falling edge makes no pulse.

21
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.167
In cycle 7 sig_q falls to 0 as well,
one cycle after sig.

22
00:01:47.167 --> 00:01:51.767
In cycle 8 sig rises again. sig_q is 0,
so a second pulse comes out.

23
00:01:51.767 --> 00:01:56.367
And in cycle 9 sig_q catches the 1,
and the pulse ends after one cycle.

24
00:01:56.767 --> 00:02:00.767
sig_q is a copy of sig, one cycle late.
The pulse: the cycles where sig = 1 and sig_q = 0.

25
00:02:01.567 --> 00:02:06.067
And this is the page's diagram, in WaveDrom:
the same four signals, the same cycles.

26
00:02:06.867 --> 00:02:12.367
In the page's box you draw sig by clicking,
and the circuit draws sig_q and pulse.

27
00:02:12.367 --> 00:02:17.033
Click cycle 2: sig rises,
and pulse is high in cycle 2 only.

28
00:02:17.033 --> 00:02:21.700
Cycle 3: sig is still high, but sig_q is already 1,
so there is no pulse.

29
00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:25.733
With cycles 4 and 5 as well:
one pulse, one cycle wide.

30
00:02:25.733 --> 00:02:29.700
A second rise in cycle 8:
another pulse, again one cycle.

31
00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.833
And cycle 9 completes the page's diagram.

32
00:02:32.833 --> 00:02:37.333
The reset trap: sig is already high
when the reset is released, in cycle 0.

33
00:02:37.333 --> 00:02:41.333
sig_q starts at 0, so a pulse comes out
in cycle 0, with no rising edge.

34
00:02:41.333 --> 00:02:45.333
If that is not wanted, reset sig_q to 1,
or filter out the first cycle.

35
00:02:45.333 --> 00:02:49.833
A follow-up: a falling edge is ~sig & sig_q,
0 now and 1 before.

36
00:02:49.833 --> 00:02:53.500
And any change, rising or falling,
is sig ^ sig_q.

37
00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:57.333
Now let's write it in SystemVerilog.

38
00:03:04.867 --> 00:03:09.367
The module rise_detect: a clock, an active-low reset,
the input sig and the output pulse.

39
00:03:10.567 --> 00:03:15.067
sig_q: all the memory of the circuit,
one bit, the sig of the previous cycle.

40
00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:26.300
always_ff: registered logic, updated
on a rising clk or a falling rst_n.

41
00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:30.300
On reset sig_q is cleared to 0,
and in every other cycle it samples sig.

42
00:03:32.533 --> 00:03:36.767
assign: the output is combinational,
exactly pulse = sig & ~sig_q.

43
00:03:37.733 --> 00:03:41.767
And that is the whole module: one flip-flop,
a NOT gate and an AND gate.

44
00:03:42.467 --> 00:03:46.000
Let's walk through the code beside the box,
from the empty diagram.

45
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.900
In cycle 2 sig = 1 and sig_q = 0,
so the assign drives pulse = 1.

46
00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:55.067
At the next clock edge: sig_q <= sig.
Now sig_q = 1.

47
00:03:55.067 --> 00:03:59.467
The assign computes again: ~sig_q = 0,
and pulse falls after one cycle.

48
00:03:59.467 --> 00:04:03.333
As long as sig stays high, sig_q stays 1
and the pulse does not come back.

49
00:04:03.333 --> 00:04:08.233
And on reset sig_q <= 0 even when sig is high:
that is the trap's pulse.

50
00:04:09.333 --> 00:04:14.800
The cost: one flip-flop and two gates.
From the input sig to the output: one gate.

51
00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:18.800
So the circuit does not limit the clock rate,
and it has no counter or state machine.

52
00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:25.100
A trap: detecting the edge with always @(posedge sig).
That turns sig into another clock.

53
00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:30.600
It gives no simple pulse one clk cycle wide.
Instead, sample sig with clk.

54
00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:36.800
A trap: sig coming from another clock domain.
First synchronize it with two flip-flops,

55
00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:42.300
and only then detect the edge. Otherwise metastability
may make double or missing pulses.

56
00:04:43.099 --> 00:04:47.899
In short: one flip-flop remembers sig,
and pulse = sig & ~sig_q.

57
00:04:47.899 --> 00:04:53.799
Try drawing sig yourself in the box
on the page of question 0001 on the site.
